    "preheader": "Six substances recommended after divided votes—and why that is not the regulatory green light sellers may claim.",
    "markdown": "# A committee voted for BPC-157. Almost nothing changed.\n\nYou subscribed for meaningful changes in the regulatory record—not manufactured\nurgency. This is the first update, and it will only arrive when something\nactually changes.\n\n**What changed:** An FDA advisory committee recommended six peptide-related\nbulk drug substances for possible inclusion on the 503A Bulks List.\n\n**What did not change:** None became FDA-approved, automatically lawful to\ncompound, or validated as retail research products.\n\n**What happens next:** FDA must decide whether to proceed through the formal\nregulatory process. The committee’s vote is advisory.\n\n---\n\n## What happened\n\nOn **23–24 July 2026**, the FDA’s Pharmacy Compounding Advisory Committee\nconsidered seven peptide-related bulk drug substances for possible inclusion on\nthe 503A Bulks List—a federal pathway that can permit qualifying substances to\nbe used in certain patient-specific pharmacy compounding when statutory and\nregulatory conditions are met.\n\nThe committee recommended:\n\n**BPC-157 · TB-500 · KPV · MOTS-c · Epitalon · Semax**\n\nIt declined to recommend emideltide, also known as DSIP.\n\nThree details matter more than the headline.\n\n**The margins were narrow.** The votes included 8–6 margins with one abstention\nfor BPC-157, TB-500 and KPV, and a 7–5 vote for MOTS-c. These were not consensus\ndecisions.\n\n**The committee voted against FDA staff recommendations.** FDA reviewers\nrecommended against inclusion, citing unresolved questions involving\ncharacterization, quality, safety and evidence of effectiveness.\n\n**Identity was part of the problem.** FDA staff raised concerns about whether\nthe substances were defined and characterized consistently enough to support a\nreliable evaluation. Before safety, effectiveness or manufacturing quality can\nbe assessed, the substance itself must be clearly identified.\n\n---\n\n## What it does not mean\n\nA committee recommendation is not a rule change.\n\nThe committee does not bind FDA. The vote did not add these substances to the\n503A Bulks List, approve them as drugs, or create broad permission to manufacture\nor sell them.\n\nAny actual change requires further FDA action through the applicable regulatory\nprocess, and the final outcome can differ from the committee’s recommendation.\n\n**Nothing about what a consumer could lawfully purchase changed on July 24.**\n\nThe vote also does not prove that a vial sold online is FDA-approved, lawful,\nclinically effective, correctly identified, sterile, accurately dosed or\nproperly manufactured. Those remain separate questions.\n\nWhen a seller presents this vote as validation—or worse, as approval—that claim\nshould be treated cautiously. It is the most predictable marketing narrative to\ncome out of the meeting.\n\n---\n\n## Why the identity issue matters\n\nFDA’s concern reflects the same analytical problem this site repeatedly\nemphasizes:\n\nYou cannot properly evaluate a substance whose identity is not pinned down.\n\nA reported purity percentage describes the sample and method used for a\nparticular test. By itself, it does not establish that the material is the\nmolecule claimed on the label, that the amount is accurate, that the product is\nsterile, or that harmful contaminants are absent.\n\nThe regulators encountered a version of the same verification problem faced by\nanyone looking at a vial and a certificate of analysis. They simply have more\nresources and better instruments.\n\n---\n\n## Also moving\n\n- **Category 2 removals** — BPC-157, TB-500 and CJC-1295 were removed from the\n  flagged-risk category in April, but were not moved into Category 1. Removal\n  from one category should not be read as permission, approval or a finding of\n  safety.\n\n- **Proposed 503B exclusions** — FDA proposed excluding semaglutide,\n  tirzepatide and liraglutide from the 503B Bulks List. The public comment\n  period closed on June 29, 2026. A final determination has not yet been\n  announced.\n\n- **Telehealth warning letters** — FDA announced 30 warning letters concerning\n  false or misleading marketing of compounded GLP-1 products, particularly\n  claims that could imply equivalence with FDA-approved drugs.\n\nRead the full regulatory tracker, with dates and sources attached to each entry:\n\n**https://in-the-vial.com/#tracker**\n\n---\n\n## One housekeeping note\n\nEvery compound page now carries citations. Material claims link to the study,\ntrial or regulatory document behind them, and cited DOIs were checked against\nthe DOI registry to confirm that they resolve to the paper identified.\n\nTwo GHK-Cu citations are worth highlighting because they do not support the\nusual promotional narrative.\n\nA controlled trial of topical GHK-Cu after laser resurfacing found **no\nobjective improvement** in wrinkles or overall skin quality, although\npatient-reported satisfaction was higher.\n\nSeveral widely cited GHK-Cu reviews were also authored by the founder of a\ncompany that sells GHK-Cu skincare. That conflict of interest does not\nautomatically invalidate the papers, but readers should know about it when the\nsame publications are presented as independent support.\n\nThe new methodology page explains who writes the site, which sources are\nsearched, how evidence tiers are assigned, and where the project’s limits are.\nIt also states that the work has no external peer review and that the author\ndoes not claim medical, pharmaceutical, laboratory or legal authority.\n\n**https://in-the-vial.com/#method**\n\n---\n\nWritten by Alexander Ayala for In The Vial.\n\n*Educational and regulatory commentary only. Nothing here is medical,\npharmaceutical or legal advice. No products, vendor links, purchase\nrecommendations or dosing guidance.*\n\n*Corrections: corrections@in-the-vial.com*\n\n*Unsubscribe: {{unsubscribe_url}}*"
